WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.910 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  so  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  hello my name is cecil slipovic and i'm  musician in residence at yale  university's  fortune of video archive for holocaust  testimonies  this video series is designed as a  companion to our album  where is our homeland which is the first  volume in the archives ongoing effort  to present songs and poems performed by  survivors in testimonies given to the  fortune of archive  over the last 40 years you can learn  more about the project on the archives  website  songs from testimonies.org  where is our homeland songs from  testimonies volume 1  was recorded in 2019 by the cecil  slupovich ensemble  featuring renowned singer sasha luria  craig udelman on the violin joshua camp  on the accordion  keyboards and guitar dmitry ischenko on  the base  and myself on woodwinds the songs in  this collection differ vastly in terms  of style  social and historical background  language and many other aspects  but collectively they create a  multi-dimensional picture  of jewish life before and during world  war  ii one of the most musically and  ethnographically compelling testimonies  in the fortune of video archive  is the one given by jack m a native of  the polish town  of shidwaviads jack had an impressive  memory  and was a great storyteller and singer  it is not worthy that jack was also  recorded for the folk song project  carried out in the 1980s jack remembered  really a wealth of the pre-war  traditional jewish repertoire  including batonis wedding jesters  recitatives  folk songs of all kinds songs in polish  some of which he learned while serving  in the army  one of the most mesmerizing either songs  jack sang on his testimony was  tribe de valesti fertig drive your waves  deep river  it is a song performed on behalf of a  woman who is longing for a beloved on  the bank of a deep river  and she asks the river to tell him about  her bitter faith  this is a pagan slavic trait which is  also referred to as folk judaism  superstitions and dialogue with the  forces of nature and other borrowings  from the para religious beliefs  of the slavic baltic finnic moldavian  and romanian neighbors  took their firm place in the jewish  folklore which shouldn't come as  surprise  given the 650 year long history of  coexistence  and this song is not an exception the  melody is typical for the eastern slavic  rural lyrical song of the 19th century  with its characteristic intrasyllabic  vocalization  and wide vocal range  she is she's singing like  she was standing by the ocean to the  waves  to instead of a letter she  those two i remember  but that was a third one but i can't  remember  [Music]  hmm  [Music]  i  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  go  [Music]  [Music]  there  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]  wow  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  oh  jack m recalls that he learned some of  the songs he knows  from hoisingeth  courtyard and street singers who would  come from warsaw  to his town and sing the latest hits  but he learned this particular song from  the bala boste  the female boss in a shop where he was  apprenticing as a tailor  she would sing songs as she was working  including lullabies in his testimony  jack compares this song to a letter  addressed  to a lover who has gone away  he remembers two verses of what must  have been a longer ballad  comparable to the well-known papillis  d'orvice  or dunden  similar to other yiddish fluff songs in  the style of this european genre  reminiscent of the russian and german  folk repertoires  the renowned folklorist ruth rubin  wrote in her 1973 books book  voices of a people that i quote  an examination of 19th century yiddish  love songs  will reveal many parallels with old  german love songs of several centuries  ago  while other con authors contain only  fragmentary evidence of this early  ancestry  the bulk of yiddish folk songs today  however relate mainly to 19th century  life of the jews in the  terrorist pale of settlement  within this framework these love songs  were the forerunners  which inspired the first generations of  yiddish lyric poets  in modern yiddish literature and of  course  as the coordinator of the ruth rubin  legacy website  i have heard many such songs  that you can also now access easily as  since close to 2 000 songs have been  digitized and are now online as part of  this  project it is notable  that this type of songs was often  transmitted  through women who um  although they were not allowed to  perform uh publicly  mostly because of religious reasons  would sing abundantly  in their homes and like in the case of  jax balaboste at work  the emotions of the songs  are conveyed through images of nature  and tribe  the mountains and valleys represent  the greetings that the singer wishes to  send to their last love  at the same time they symbolize the very  distance  separating the singer from their loved  ones  while the ways can be seen as reflecting  the agitated emotional state caused  by their heartbreak and longing  as the joke goes if you find yourself  in a yiddish love song stay away from  the river  the same could be said of other european  folk songs  where desperate lovers sometimes end up  drowning themselves  although this song doesn't present such  a tragic  ending the topography of the feelings  in the last two lines convey bitterness  through the idea of depth whether it is  the valley  the river or the image of darkness  in their arrangement of this song the  thistle sleepovich ensemble  and sasha lurie have created a texture  with drone-like accompaniment in unison  in the melody which emphasizes both  the lyricism of the text  and the underlying darkness expressed  within  it's a beautiful work

